<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Yawnghwe State</h1><p class="st-meta">1359 CE – 1959 CE (approx.) · capital Yawnghwe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Shan state of Inle Lake, whose last ruler, Sao Shwe Thaike, became the first president of independent Burma.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Yawnghwe, Burmese Nyaungshwe, lay at the northern end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inle-lake">Inle Lake</a> in the southern Shan plateau. Shan tradition dates its ruling house to 1359, when two brothers are said to have settled the area with Burmese royal approval. It became one of the leading southern Shan states.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its saophas ruled a lake and valley economy of floating gardens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and fishing worked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intha">Intha</a>, Shan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pao-people">Pa-O</a> and Danu peoples. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaung-daw-oo-pagoda">Phaung Daw Oo pagoda</a> on the lake, with its five Buddha images carried in an annual boat procession, was a major centre of devotion. Yawnghwe paid tribute to Ava and later to the Konbaung kings.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under British rule Yawnghwe became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federated-shan-states">Federated Shan States</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-shwe-thaike">Sao Shwe Thaike</a>, its saopha, served as the first President of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Union of Burma</a> from 1948 to 1952. The rulers renounced their powers in 1959, and Sao Shwe Thaike died in prison after the 1962 coup.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yawnghwe-state.jpg" alt="Yawnghwe State" width="320" height="368"><figcaption>Image: User:Jolle · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shan_States-Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1359 CE – 1959 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yawnghwe</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sao Shwe Thaike</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Myanmar</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yawnghwe, Nyaungshwe</dd></dl></aside></div>

